            "We now turn our attention to the second pillar of operational equipment excellence: Shigeo Shingo's legendary methodology for slashing machine changeover times: Single-Minute Exchange of Die, universally known as SMED. In conventional mass-production manufacturing, equipment changeovers are universally treated as long, unavoidable, and highly disruptive events. Changing an eighty-ton stamping die in an automotive body press or re-tooling an injection molding machine historically consumed four to eight hours of non-productive downtime. Because changeovers were so costly, plant managers justified producing enormous batch sizes of tens of thousands of parts to 'amortize the setup cost over more units.' This created massive mountains of stagnant finished goods and WIP inventory, inflating lead times and suffocating operational agility.",
