            "Stage One is the crucial conceptual leap: Separating Internal and External Setup. The team rigorously audits every task on the video log: could this task have been performed while the previous run was operating? By strictly enforcing that all tooling, dies, materials, fixtures, clamps, documentation, and quality measuring gauges must be pre-staged at the machine cell before the line stops, changeover downtime is instantly cut by thirty to fifty percent without spending a single dollar on engineering modifications.",
            "Stage Two focuses on Converting Internal Setup to External Setup. The engineering team examines remaining internal tasks and invents mechanisms to shift them outside the stoppage window. For example, in plastic injection molding, heating a mold to operating temperature (180 degrees Celsius) historically consumed 45 minutes of internal press time. Engineers construct an external pre-heating staging station adjacent to the press. The incoming mold is brought to operating temperature electrically while the previous job is running; when the press stops, the hot mold is slid into place, eliminating 45 minutes of internal heating delay. Similarly, optical tool presetters allow CNC cutting tools to be measured and offset values loaded into the machine controller while the machine is actively cutting parts.",
